.\" and every option the program takes, and only those, is named here.
.TH ACORNDW 1 2026-11-24 "Acorn Disk Wrangler" "User Commands"
.nh
.SH NAME
acorndw \- list, extract, add and change files on Acorn disc and tape images
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B acorndw
.RI [ "global options" ]
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.IR arguments ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B acorndw
is the console version of Acorn Disk Wrangler: the same detection, the same checks and the same checked save as the
window, with no display needed, so it works in scripts.
It reads and writes:
.IP \(bu 2
ADFS hard discs (old map, BeebSCSI
.I scsi0.dat
and similar) and ADFS S, M and L floppies
.RI ( .ads ,
.IR .adm ,
.IR .adl );
.IP \(bu
DFS discs, single- or double-sided, 40 or 80 track
.RI ( .ssd ,
.IR .dsd ),
the two sides of a double-sided disc being two separate discs;
.IP \(bu
Acorn DOS Plus 800K discs, the Master 512's 640K ADFS/DOS Plus hybrid (its ADFS side read-only), and PC floppies
(FAT12: 360K, 720K, 1.2M, 1.44M);
.IP \(bu
BBC Micro and Electron cassette tapes
.RI ( .uef ,
compressed or not).
.PP
A floppy may also be in an ImageDisk
.RI ( .imd ),
DSK or extended DSK file, or a TeleDisk
.RI ( .td0 )
file, which can only be read.
.PP
Global options may come before or after the command.
An image and a path on it are always separate arguments
.RB ( "acorndw get games.ssd \(aq$.ELITE\(aq out/" ),
never
.IR image : path .
Quote paths that start with
.B $
or
.B #
from the shell.
.PP
Each run opens the image, makes its changes as one transaction and saves once: every filesystem is checked first,
the new file is written beside the old one and read back, then put in its place.
If anything fails, the image is left exactly as it was.
.SS Paths
Paths are written as Acorn's machines write them.
ADFS:
.B $.Games.Elite
or
.B Games.Elite
(a dot separates the levels;
.B /
is an ordinary character in a name).
DFS:
.B $.ELITE
or
.BR A.PROG ,
the letter before the dot being the file's directory.
DOS and FAT:
.B /GAMES/ELITE.COM
(either slash).
Tape:
.B GAME
or
.BR #3 ,
the third file on the tape; a second file called
.B GAME
is also
.BR "GAME (2)" .
The wildcards
.B *
and
.B ?\&
expand in
.BR get ,
.BR rm ,
.B attr
and
.BR ls .
.SS Sidecar files
Files extracted from ADFS, DFS and tapes get an
.I .inf
file beside them holding the name, load and exec addresses and attributes, and
.B put
reads them back, as other Acorn tools do.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Write one JSON document on standard output (see
.BR "JSON OUTPUT" )
and nothing else there.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Do everything, including every check, but write nothing.
.TP
.BI \-\-vol= NAME
Which volume of the image:
.BR adfs ,
.B dos
(a hybrid disc's DOS side, or a hard disc's DOS Plus partition),
.B drive0
and
.B drive2
(the two sides of a double-sided DFS disc),
.BR tape .
.B all
lists every volume, for
.B ls
and
.BR info .
.TP
.BI \-\-fs= ID
Read the image as this filesystem, whatever detection says
.RB ( info
lists the readings and their ids).
.TP
.B \-\-backup
Keep the old file as
.IB name .bak
when saving.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
No output on success.
.TP
.BI \-\-sides= auto | interleaved | sequential
The side order of a double-sided image: track by track, both sides
.RB ( interleaved ),
or all of side 0, then all of side 1
.RB ( sequential ).
.B auto
(the default) works it out.
On
.BR new ,
the order to make.
.TP
.BR \-f " \fINAME\fR, " \-\-format= \fINAME\fR
On
.BR new ,
the format to make (one of those
.B new \-\-list
gives).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the commands and global options;
.IB command " \-\-help"
prints that command's own options.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and the DiskWranglerCore version.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS info
.B acorndw info
.I image
.PP
What the image is: the container, the detection result and the other readings, a double-sided disc's side order
and how it was found, and each volume with its title and free space.
.SS check
.B acorndw check
.I image
.RB [ \-\-strict ]
.PP
Check every filesystem on the image.
Exit status 4 if one fails;
.B \-\-strict
counts warnings as failures too.
.SS ls
.B acorndw ls
.I image
.RI [ path ]
.RB [ \-R ]
.RB [ \-d ]
.PP
List a directory: name, type
.RB ( D
for a directory), attributes, size and date.
ADFS shows
.B L
(locked),
.B W
(write),
.B R
(read),
.B E
(execute only); DFS
.BR L ;
FAT
.B R
(read-only),
.B H
(hidden),
.B S
(system),
.B A
(archive).
A tape is listed in its own order, not sorted.
.BR \-R ", " \-\-recursive
lists every directory below the path;
.BR \-d ", " \-\-directory
shows the path itself.
.SS get
.B acorndw get
.I image path... hostdir
.PP
Extract files and directories into the host folder
.IR hostdir ,
or, with
.B \-
as
.IR hostdir ,
write one file to standard output.
.RS
.TP
.BI \-\-sidecars= inf | none
Write an
.I .inf
file beside each file (the default), or none.
.TP
.BI \-\-manifest= FILE
Also write a retro-files manifest of what was extracted.
.TP
.BR \-\-replace ", " \-\-skip
What to do with a host file of the same name: replace it, or leave it and go on.
Without either, the run stops with exit status 5.
.RE
.SS put
.B acorndw put
.I image host... dir
.PP
Add host files and folders to the directory
.I dir
.RB ( $
for the root).
Names are made valid for the filesystem; a name that clashes within one
.B put
gets
.BR ~1 ,
.BR ~2 ...
On a tape the files go on its end; a name already on the tape is no clash, only a file identical to one there is.
.RS
.TP
.BI \-\-sidecars= inf | none
Read
.I .inf
files for load and exec addresses and attributes (the default), or ignore them.
.TP
.BR \-\-replace ", " \-\-skip
What to do when a file of that name is there (on a tape: the first file of that name).
Without either, the run stops with exit status 5.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Replace a locked file too.
.TP
.BI \-\-retro= FILE
Take names, addresses and attributes from a retro-files manifest or JSON file (the drag-and-drop format shared with
CP/M Disk Wrangler and FLEX Disk Wrangler); may be given more than once.
.TP
.BI \-\-letters= dirs | flatten
DFS files arriving on ADFS: a file's directory letter becomes an ADFS directory
.RB ( dirs ,
the default:
.B P.GAME
arrives as
.BR $.P.GAME ),
or is dropped
.RB ( flatten :
.BR $.GAME ).
.RE
.SS rm
.B acorndw rm
.I image path...
.RB [ \-r ]
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.PP
Delete files.
.BR \-r ", " \-\-recursive
deletes directories with everything in them;
.B \-\-force
deletes locked files too.
On a tape, files are removed last first.
.SS mkdir
.B acorndw mkdir
.I image path...
.RB [ \-p ]
.PP
Make ADFS or FAT directories;
.BR \-p ", " \-\-parents
makes the ones above too.
.SS mv
.B acorndw mv
.I image from to
.PP
Rename a file or directory, or move it to another directory where the filesystem can (on DFS, to another directory
letter).
.SS attr
.B acorndw attr
.I image path...
.RB [ \-\-set
.IR key = value ]...
.PP
Show a file's attributes, or change them with
.BR \-\-set ,
which may be given more than once.
The keys are, on ADFS:
.BR read ,
.BR write ,
.BR locked ,
.BR execute ,
.BR load ,
.BR exec ;
on DFS:
.BR locked ,
.BR load ,
.BR exec ;
on FAT:
.BR readonly ,
.BR hidden ,
.BR system ,
.BR archive ;
on a tape:
.BR load ,
.BR exec ,
.BR protected .
Addresses are hexadecimal; yes/no values are
.BR yes / no ,
.BR on / off ,
.BR true / false
or
.BR 1 / 0 .
An unknown key is refused with the list of the filesystem's keys.
.SS title
.B acorndw title
.I image
.RI [ text ]
.PP
Show or set the disc title (ADFS, DFS) or volume label (FAT).
Tapes have none (exit status 6).
.SS boot
.B acorndw boot
.I image
.RI [ option ]
.PP
Show or set the boot option of an ADFS or DFS disc:
.B 0
(off),
.B 1
(*LOAD !BOOT),
.B 2
(*RUN !BOOT) or
.B 3
(*EXEC !BOOT).
.SS new
.B acorndw new
.I file
.BI \-\-format= ID
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B acorndw new \-\-list
.PP
Make a blank disc, or an empty tape
.RB ( \-\-format=uef ).
The disc is checked and opened again before it is kept.
Discs are made as raw images; to make an ImageDisk or DSK file,
.B convert
the new one.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-list
List the formats.
.TP
.BI \-\-title= TEXT
The disc title.
.TP
.BI \-\-dosboot= FILE
The boot code for the
.B dos\-640
hybrid disc, from a real Master 512 boot disc (Acorn's code can't be built in, so this format needs it).
.TP
.BI \-\-container= KIND
.B raw
only (see above).
.TP
.B \-\-replace
Replace the file if it exists.
.RE
.SS convert
.B acorndw convert
.I image
.RI [ out ]
.RB [ \-\-container= \fIKIND\fR]
.RB [ \-\-to\-sides= \fIORDER\fR]
.PP
Write a floppy in another container
.RB ( raw ,
.BR imd ,
.B dsk
or
.BR edsk ),
to
.I out
or over the image.
TeleDisk files can't be written, so convert one to change it.
.RS
.TP
.BI \-\-to\-sides= interleaved | sequential
Change a double-sided raw image's side order (in place, without
.IR out ).
.TP
.B \-\-replace
Replace
.I out
if it exists.
.RE
.SS copy
.B acorndw copy
.I src dest path...
.RB [ \-\-into= \fIDIR\fR]
.RB [ \-\-to\-vol= \fINAME\fR]
.PP
Copy files from one image to another (or within one), mapping names, addresses and attributes as drag and drop
does: disc to tape, tape to disc, DFS to ADFS, and so on.
.B \-\-into
names the directory they go to,
.B \-\-to\-vol
the volume of
.IR dest .
.BR \-\-replace ,
.BR \-\-skip ,
.B \-\-force
and
.B \-\-letters
are as for
.BR put .
.SS script
.B acorndw script
.I image
.RI [ file | \- ]
.PP
Run many commands on one image with one save.
Each line of
.I file
(or standard input) is a command without
.B acorndw
and the image
.RB ( "put host/ELITE $" );
lines starting
.B #
are comments.
Global options go on the
.B script
command itself;
.BR new ,
.B convert
and
.B script
can't be used in a script.
It stops at the first line that fails, and then nothing is saved.
.SH JSON OUTPUT
With
.BR \-\-json ,
standard output is one JSON document (schema 1):
.BR schema ,
.BR tool ,
.BR command ,
.BR image ,
.BR vol ,
.BR ok ,
.BR exit ,
.BR dryRun ,
.BR saved ,
.B result
(which depends on the command),
.B changes
(one per file added, deleted, renamed or changed),
.B warnings
and
.B error
(null, or
.BR code ,
.BR kind ,
.B message
and
.BR path ).
Scripts should read
.B exit
for the outcome and the rest for detail.
Text output is for people and may change between versions; the JSON only gains fields.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP 4
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The operation failed (the disc is full, a bad name, a file that can't be read).
.TP
.B 2
Wrong arguments.
.TP
.B 3
The image, a path on it, or a host file isn't there.
.TP
.B 4
The disc fails its check, so nothing was written.
.TP
.B 5
A name exists and neither
.B \-\-replace
nor
.B \-\-skip
was given.
.TP
.B 6
Unsupported, or read-only: a TeleDisk file, a hybrid disc's ADFS side, something the filesystem can't do (a move
between FAT directories).
.TP
.B 7
The disc belongs to another Disk Wrangler (a CP/M or FLEX disc;
.B info
names it).
.TP
.B 8
Two readings are equally likely: choose one with
.BR \-\-fs .
.TP
.B 9
Nothing recognised the disc
.RB ( \-\-fs
tries a reading anyway).
.TP
.B 10
The checked save failed; the original file is untouched.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH
If set, the time used for FAT entries and ImageDisk headers, so that a run gives the same bytes every time.
.SH EXAMPLES
In a folder holding the files
.I HELLO
and
.IR ELITE :
.PP
.EX
acorndw new games.ssd \-\-format=dfs\-ss80      # a blank 80\-track DFS disc
acorndw put games.ssd HELLO ELITE \(aq$\(aq
acorndw ls games.ssd
acorndw get games.ssd ELITE out/              # out/ELITE and out/ELITE.inf
acorndw check games.ssd
acorndw convert games.ssd games.imd \-\-container=imd
acorndw \-\-dry\-run rm games.ssd \(aq$.HELLO\(aq
acorndw get game.uef \(aq#3\(aq out/              # the third file on a tape
acorndw copy game.uef games.ssd GAME          # a tape file onto a disc
acorndw \-\-json ls games.ssd
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR acorndiskwrangler ,
the program with a window;
.BR cpmdw (1)
and
.BR flexdw (1),
the other Disk Wranglers' console programs.
.PP
